    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:privacy-range",
      "thm:private-upper-envelope",
      "thm:private-lower-envelope",
      "lem:nonprivate-oracle-bump-lower",
      "def:phase-map"
    ],
    "justification": "The explicit phase map states exactly where the new nuisance privacy price changes the attainable risk.",
    "gap": "The cited CATE and private-regression papers each have elbows, but neither derives this causal nuisance-by-privacy phase diagram.",
    "consumer": "It tells analysts when privacy, rather than the unresolved nonprivate exact-uniform difficulty, determines sample size.",
    "proof_tex": "The private lower envelope and private upper envelope give\n\\[\n c\\max\\{R_n^{\\mathrm{NP}},\\rho_n^{\\mathrm{priv}}\\}\n \\le R_n^{\\mathrm{DP}}\n \\le C\\max\\{b_n,\\rho_n^{\\mathrm{priv}}\\}.                 \\tag{19}\n\\]\nIf \\(\\rho_n^{\\mathrm{priv}}\\ge c_1b_n\\), the right side of (19) is at most a constant times \\(\\rho_n^{\\mathrm{priv}}\\), so the bounds match.  If \\(q\\ge q_0=d\\gamma/(2\\gamma+d)\\), then\n\\[\n \\frac{d}{q}\\le2+\\frac d\\gamma,\n \\qquad\n \\kappa=\\frac{2}{2+d/\\gamma+d/q}\\ge\\frac{\\gamma}{2\\gamma+d}=\\nu.\n\\]\nThus \\(b_n=n^{-\\nu}\\), and the direct nonprivate bump lemma gives \\(b_n\\le C R_n^{\\mathrm{NP}}\\).  Equation (19) again matches, proving the first assertion.\n\nNow write \\(\\epsilon_n=n^{-t}\\).  The privacy range gives \\(0\\le t\\le1\\), and\n\\[\n r_G=n^{-(1-t)\\gamma/(\\gamma+d)},\n \\qquad r_F=n^{-(2-t)/D_F}.                              \\tag{20}\n\\]\nThe two private exponents in (20) agree precisely when\n\\[\n \\frac{2-t}{1+d/\\gamma+d/q}\n =\\frac{1-t}{1+d/\\gamma},\n\\]\nwhich rearranges to\n\\[\n t=t_s=1-\\frac qd-\\frac q\\gamma.                         \\tag{21}\n\\]\nAlso \\(r_G=n^{-\\nu}\\) at \\(t=\\nu\\), while \\(r_F=n^{-\\kappa}\\) at \\(t=\\kappa\\), because \\(\\kappa=2/(D_F+1)\\).\n\nWhen \\(q=q_0\\), direct substitution gives \\(\\kappa=t_s=\\nu\\).  When \\(q>q_0\\), monotonicity in \\(q\\) gives \\(\\kappa>\\nu\\) and \\(t_s<\\nu\\); hence both privacy terms are no larger than \\(b_n=n^{-\\nu}\\) for \\(t\\le\\nu\\), and \\(r_G\\) exceeds both \\(b_n\\) and \\(r_F\\) for \\(t>\\nu\\).  This proves the first phase partition and its equality point.\n\nWhen \\(q<q_0\\), the inequalities reverse: \\(\\kappa<\\nu\\) and \\(t_s>\\kappa\\).  Here \\(b_n=n^{-\\kappa}\\).  Equation (20) shows that both privacy terms are at most \\(b_n\\) for \\(t\\le\\kappa\\); \\(r_F\\) is largest for \\(\\kappa<t<t_s\\); (21) gives equality at \\(t_s\\); and \\(r_G\\) is largest for \\(t>t_s\\).  Since \\(q>0\\), one has \\(0<\\kappa<t_s<1\\), so the listed intervals exhaust the privacy range.",
